---
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import ArticleFaq from '../../../components/ArticleFaq.astro';
import CTASection from '../../../components/CTASection.astro';
import { MANUFACTURER, CERTIFICATIONS, RELATIONSHIP } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'Choosing an OEM Battery Manufacturer | Dingwei';
const description = 'A practical method for choosing an OEM battery manufacturer: standards, certifications, factory capability, sampling and export terms.';
const articleTitle = 'How to choose an OEM battery manufacturer';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the single most important factor when choosing a manufacturer?', a: 'Repeatability — whether the tenth container matches the first approved sample. A low price is worth nothing if the specification drifts between orders.' },
  { q: 'Should I choose the lowest price?', a: 'No. Price only matters when the specification, certification and repeatability are equal. A cheaper quote that skips the CCA basis or terminal detail is not actually comparable.' },
  { q: 'How do I verify a factory without visiting it?', a: 'Ask for three things: current certificates, a recent production sample, and their CCA test procedure. A supplier that cannot produce all three is a risk regardless of the brochure.' },
];
---
<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} articleTitle={articleTitle} articleSection="Procurement" dateModified="2026-09-03" faqs={faqs}>
  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge</span>
    <h1>How to choose an OEM battery manufacturer</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-01 · Source: Chengguang Energy technical documentation</p>
    <p class="lead">Choose a manufacturer that can match your standard, hold quality systems, and repeat your specification order after order.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> The single most important factor in choosing an OEM battery manufacturer is repeatability — whether the tenth container matches the first approved sample. Match the standard family, verify current certificates and check output against your volume, but a low price is worth nothing if the specification drifts between orders.
    </div>

    <h2>1. Match the standard family</h2>
    <p>Your market determines the standard: JIS, DIN, BCI, AS or SA. Confirm the factory regularly produces that family rather than treating your order as a one-off.</p>

    <h2>2. Check quality systems</h2>
    <p>Ask which certifications the manufacturer holds. For automotive supply, IATF 16949 is the relevant quality system; ISO 9001 covers general quality management.</p>
    <p>{MANUFACTURER.name} holds IATF 16949, ISO 9001, ISO 45001, OHSAS 18001 and CE.</p>

    <h2>3. Verify factory capability</h2>
    <p>Look at plant size, line count, daily output, export experience and whether the facility can support your volume. {MANUFACTURER.name} operates a {MANUFACTURER.factoryArea} facility with {MANUFACTURER.productionLines}, producing {MANUFACTURER.dailyOutput} and exporting to {MANUFACTURER.exportMarkets}.</p>

    <h2>4. Define the specification before quoting</h2>
    <p>Fix voltage, capacity, CCA, case size, terminal and polarity first. Ambiguous specifications produce samples that cannot be fairly compared.</p>
    <p><a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-specification-guide/">OEM battery specification guide &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2>5. Evaluate sampling and terms</h2>
    <p>Confirm the sampling step, lead time, payment and warranty before committing. {RELATIONSHIP}</p>
    <p><a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-supplier-checklist/">OEM battery supplier checklist &rarr;</a> · <a href="/oem/">OEM capability &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2>A five-point decision framework</h2>
    <p class="lead">Score each manufacturer against the same five questions instead of comparing marketing claims.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Specification</strong> — &ldquo;Which standards and models do you produce in-house?&rdquo;</li>
      <li><strong>Certification</strong> — &ldquo;Can you share current IATF 16949 / ISO 9001 certificates?&rdquo;</li>
      <li><strong>Scale</strong> — &ldquo;What is your daily output and how does it map to my annual volume?&rdquo;</li>
      <li><strong>Repeatability</strong> — &ldquo;How do you lock a specification so repeat orders match?&rdquo;</li>
      <li><strong>Export</strong> — &ldquo;Which markets do you ship to, and what documents do you provide?&rdquo;</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Combine this framework with the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-specification-guide/">OEM battery specification guide</a> so you are scoring the same requirements you will actually order.</p>

    <h2>Questions that expose a weak supplier</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>They cannot name the CCA test method.</li>
      <li>They quote before confirming the standard family.</li>
      <li>They cannot show a sample from a recent production run.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Run each shortlisted factory through the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-supplier-checklist/">OEM battery supplier checklist</a> before you commit volume.</p>

    <h2>Weight the five factors</h2>
    <p>Do not choose on one number. Score every shortlisted manufacturer against the same five factors and total the result.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Weight</th><th>What to score</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Specification coverage</td><td>25%</td><td>Can they produce the standard family and models you need?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Certifications</td><td>25%</td><td>Are current IATF 16949 / ISO 9001 certificates available?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scale</td><td>20%</td><td>Does daily output map to your annual volume?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Repeatability</td><td>20%</td><td>Is there a locked specification and sample process?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Export experience</td><td>10%</td><td>Which markets do they serve and document?</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>{MANUFACTURER.name} holds {CERTIFICATIONS.join(', ')}, exports to {MANUFACTURER.exportMarkets} and operates {MANUFACTURER.productionLines}, so its answers can be scored in the same way as any candidate supplier. Then run the result through the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-supplier-checklist/">supplier checklist</a> before committing volume.</p>

    <h2>Lead-acid-specific checks</h2>
    <p>For lead-acid, the highest-risk checks are process checks, not marketing checks:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Grid alloy</strong> — maintenance-free batteries normally use lead-calcium grids; confirm the alloy is stated.</li>
      <li><strong>Paste consistency</strong> — ask how paste is controlled between batches.</li>
      <li><strong>Curing</strong> — verify the plate curing window is sufficient, normally 2–4 days.</li>
      <li><strong>Formation</strong> — confirm the battery is fully formed and 100% end-of-line tested.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These are the same checks a buyer should walk through on a <a href="/knowledge/how-audit-battery-factory/">lead-acid factory audit</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <ArticleFaq faqs={faqs} />

  <CTASection title="Evaluate Dingwei Battery as your manufacturer" />
</ArticleLayout>
